---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Example from "../../components/Example.astro";
import ApiTable from "../../components/ApiTable.astro";
import Code from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Code.astro";
import Stability from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Stability.astro";
import { u } from "../../data/nav";

const tabs = `<nav class="ply-cluster" style="--ply-cluster-gap: 0.25rem" aria-label="Example chapters">
  <a class="ply-tab" href="#" aria-current="page">Research</a>
  <a class="ply-tab" href="#">Teaching</a>
  <a class="ply-tab" href="#">Notes</a>
  <a class="ply-tab" href="#">CV</a>
</nav>`;
const side = `<nav class="ply-sidenav" aria-label="Example sections">
  <p class="ply-sidenav__heading">Foundations</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#">Color</a></li>
    <li><a href="#" aria-current="page">Typography</a>
      <div class="ply-toc">
        <ul aria-label="On this page">
          <li><a href="#" aria-current="true">Typefaces</a></li>
          <li><a href="#">Scale</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li><a href="#">Motion</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>`;
const crumb = `<ol class="ply-breadcrumb">
  <li><a href="#">Research</a></li>
  <li><a href="#">2026</a></li>
  <li aria-current="page">Higher-order message passing</li>
</ol>`;
const pager = `<nav class="ply-pager" aria-label="Pages" style="margin-top:0">
  <a href="#" rel="prev"><span>Previous</span><strong>Typography</strong></a>
  <a class="ply-pager__next" href="#" rel="next"><span>Next</span><strong>Motion</strong></a>
</nav>`;
---
<Docs title="Navigation" status="stable" lede="A black wall label marks the chapter you are in; a tomato point marks the page.">
  <h2>Top bar</h2>
  <p>The top bar of this site is the component: wordmark at left, chapter tabs, tools at right, a 3px rule underneath. Where the tabs do not fit, they first set closer, then the search field drops its word; where they still do not fit, they fold into a Menu button that opens a native <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> panel. <code>js/topbar.js</code> measures the fit, so a site with three chapters keeps its tabs on a phone and one with nine folds them on a small laptop. Without the script the tabs fold below 72rem, and the menu still works.</p>
  <p>The script also closes the menu when it should get out of the way: <kbd>Esc</kbd> closes it and puts focus back on its button, and a press outside it, or focus moving on past it, closes it too.</p>
  <p>The theme toggle flips light and dark and keeps the choice in <code>localStorage</code> under <code>ply-theme</code> ("light" or "dark", no identifier), so the next page opens in it. Say so in a privacy notice. <code>&lt;ThemeToggle persist={"{false}"} /&gt;</code> keeps the choice for the page only and clears a stored one; in plain HTML, add <code>data-ply-persist="false"</code> to the toggle.</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<TopBar brand="John Doe" brandHref="/" links={[
  { label: "Research", href: "/research/", current: true },
  { label: "Teaching", href: "/teaching/" },
]}>
  <ThemeToggle />
</TopBar>`} />
  <ApiTable rows={[["brand", "Wordmark text", "Polytope"], ["brandHref", "Wordmark link", "/"], ["links", "Chapters: { label, href, current }"], ["default slot", "Tools on the right: Search, the theme toggle"]]} head={["Prop", "Effect", "Default"]} />

  <h2>Search</h2>
  <p>The search in this site's top bar is the component. It opens a panel over the page with results from the site's own <a href="https://pagefind.app/">Pagefind</a> index, a static search that needs no server. Press <kbd>/</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> (<kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> on a Mac) to open it, the arrow keys to move through results, <kbd>Enter</kbd> to follow one, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> to close it. Without JavaScript the button is a plain link to a page you choose.</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<TopBar brand="John Doe" brandHref="/" links={links}>
  <Search href="/publications/" placeholder="Search papers, talks, and notes"
    links={[{ label: "Publications", href: "/publications/" }, { label: "Teaching", href: "/teaching/" }]} />
  <ThemeToggle />
</TopBar>`} />
  <p>Build the index after the site build, and mark what it should read: the main content of each page, and the kind of each page, which the panel shows beside the result. Navigation, footers, and forms are left out on their own.</p>
  <Code lang="sh" code={`npx astro build && npx pagefind --site dist`} />
  <Code lang="html" code={`<main data-pagefind-body>
  <article data-pagefind-meta="kind:Publications">...</article>
</main>`} />
  <ApiTable rows={[
    ["href", "Where the button goes without JavaScript", "#"],
    ["label, placeholder", "The button's text and the field's placeholder", "Search"],
    ["bundle", "Folder of the Pagefind files, with the site's base path", "/pagefind/"],
    ["baseUrl", "Prefix for result links when the site lives under a base path", "/"],
    ["hint, links", "A line and quick links shown before the reader types"],
    ["ranking", "Pagefind ranking options, such as { pageLength: 0.2 } so short records do not crowd out pages"],
  ]} head={["Prop", "Effect", "Default"]} />
  <p>A record can carry <code>meta.summary</code>, which the panel shows in place of an excerpt. These docs add every token, class, and component that way, from a short script that uses Pagefind's Node API after the build.</p>

  <h2>Chapter tabs</h2>
  <Example html={tabs} />
  <p>Mark the current tab with <code>aria-current="page"</code>; the black label comes from that attribute, so the visual state and the announced state cannot disagree.</p>

  <h2>Sidebar</h2>
  <Example html={side} />
  <p>The page's own sections hang under the current page, on a hairline. With Astro, put them in the <code>current</code> slot, and the auto list fills itself:</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<SideNav groups={groups}>
  <div slot="current" class="ply-toc" data-auto><ul aria-label="On this page"></ul></div>
</SideNav>`} />

  <h2>On this page</h2>
  <p>The list under the current page in this page's sidebar, and in the rail at the left of an <a href={u("/foundations/layout/#articles")}>article</a>. Give it <code>data-auto</code> and an empty <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, put <code>data-toc-scope</code> on the article, and the script fills it from the h2 and h3 headings and follows your scroll. With Astro and Markdown headings, <code>&lt;Toc headings={"{headings}"} /&gt;</code> renders it on the server instead.</p>

  <h2>Breadcrumb</h2>
  <Example html={crumb} />

  <h2>Pager</h2>
  <Example html={pager} />
  <p>A label with a subtitle, such as a paper's "Name: what it does", shows the name and keeps the rest for screen readers, so a long title does not outweigh the page; <code>split={"{false}"}</code> shows it whole. The labels balance across their lines. Where "Previous" and "Next" are not the right words, as on a list newest first, rename them.</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<Pager label="More publications" prev={{ label: newer.title, href: newer.href }} next={{ label: older.title, href: older.href }}
  prevText="Newer" nextText="Older" />`} />
</Docs>
